WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:09.240
MANVINDER SINGH:
Welcome to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.400
live webinars with Google Cloud.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.250
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:15.460
My name is Manvinder Singh.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:16.890
I'm part of the
Google Cloud team.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.361
And with me is Alim
Karim from NetApp.

00:00:19.361 --> 00:00:21.360
And we're going to be
talking about NetApp Cloud

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.910
Volumes for GCP, a
new storage service

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.100
that you can use with
your GCP workloads.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.500
So before we get
started, just a reminder,

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.570
you can ask questions
at any point of time.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:32.680
Just use the platform.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.470
And we have
colleagues standing by

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.560
to help you answer
those questions.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.610
All right, so let's get started.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.000
Some of you may have
seen last month,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.156
we announced our strategic
partnership with NetApp.

00:00:46.156 --> 00:00:48.280
And we announced that we're
collaborating to launch

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.870
NetApp Cloud Volumes for GCP.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.550
This is a new storage service.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.930
It's a high-performance service
that offers multiple protocols.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.170
It offers NFS, SMB.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:03.420
And if you use GCP
for your workloads,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.260
you can use their
storage service

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.190
while taking advantage of
various advanced management

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.890
features like data
synchronization, and snapshots,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:14.040
and cloning with your workloads.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.670
And in today's session,
we'll talk more about

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.767
what this service is, when to
use it, what kind of use case

00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:25.220
is, and how to use
the service, as well.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.610
So just to talk quickly
about the agenda,

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.020
we'll start by
talking about when

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.700
to use NetApp Cloud Volumes.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.140
We'll talk about
the architecture,

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.339
and what kind of use cases you
can use this for, and also talk

00:01:35.339 --> 00:01:37.630
about when it's going to be
available in which regions,

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:38.630
and so forth.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:41.890
And then finally, we'll
end with live Q and A.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.090
With that said,
let's get started.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.280
Alim, talk to us
about when can we

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.070
use Cloud Volumes,
what kind of use cases.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:48.910
ALIM KARIM: Yeah.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:52.630
So we initially, before
we designed and developed

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.750
Cloud Volumes, we spent a lot
of time talking to customers,

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.330
and really understanding
the context of file

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.690
and unstructured workloads as
it related to public cloud.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.400
And when we looked at the data
after all of that analysis,

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.550
we found that you could
group these problems

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.630
into three main areas--

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.240
and three main areas
and three main personas.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.200
So first, as the
slide shows, is you

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.710
have a set of application owners
and application administrators

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.500
that are trying to migrate
existing applications

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.630
from on-premise to the cloud.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:26.170
And these applications are built
around requiring POSIX file

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:26.756
services.

00:02:26.756 --> 00:02:28.630
And it's very difficult
to re-architect them,

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:31.480
so the easiest way is usually
to take them as they are,

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.300
with minimal re-architecture,
and move them to the cloud.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.550
And the reasons
behind these vary.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.820
Perhaps the customer is moving
out of a physical data center.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.520
Perhaps they're trying to
map the application that

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.242
has a variable business
process, and they

00:02:44.242 --> 00:02:46.450
want to take advantage of
the elasticity of the cloud

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:46.949
to do it.

00:02:46.949 --> 00:02:49.690
So the use case is actually
vary behind the [? why ?]

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:51.370
on the migrate side.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.580
The second is a set of
developers and DevOps folks

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.120
that are trying to build new
applications in the cloud that

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.130
require shared file semantics.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:01.900
And a quick example
of this, as you know,

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.399
you're trying to
set up a Kubernetes

00:03:03.399 --> 00:03:06.700
cluster that could have
thousands of containers in it.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.980
And all of those
containers require access

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.340
to a shared file system.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.070
So that's another big use
case that we're seeing.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:16.120
And then finally, we have
data analysts, data engineers,

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:19.690
that have a lot of
unstructured data on premise

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:21.370
that they want to
take to Google Cloud

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:23.560
to take advantage of
the advanced machine

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.300
learning and
analytics capabilities

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.790
that the Cloud offer, to
be able to get value out

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:29.330
of that unstructured data.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:32.330
So those are the
three broad use cases.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:35.440
Now, common amongst these use
cases on the right-hand side

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:36.750
is really the expectation.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.360
So all of these personas really
have three main expectations

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:44.240
around any service that
satisfies these problems.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.390
The first is this
notion of experience.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:49.740
And experience is not just about
performance and scalability.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.180
It's also about how well
the service is really

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.000
integrated into GCP,
and integrated not

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.820
just from control plane, but
also from a support and billing

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:59.210
perspective.

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:01.000
The other two pieces
are on consumption

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.120
and economics really
go hand in hand.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.590
So being a true cloud
service, customers

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:09.100
expect this to be able to
ramp up their consumption.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.500
And the economics of that
go up with consumption.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.240
And as the consumption
comes down,

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.950
their economics goes down.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:18.250
And then the final piece is,
within any cloud context,

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.649
the ability to have a
high degree of security

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:22.420
and compliance as
part of the service.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:28.410
So now let's talk
about Cloud Volumes

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.780
for Google, what they are,
and the various elements

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.280
that go with them.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.560
So the simplest way to
think about Cloud Volumes

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.380
is that they are a
no-ops managed service.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:41.460
They offer our customers a
simple-to-consume performant

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.070
NFS or SMB endpoint.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:47.910
And all the customer has to
do is consume that endpoint,

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:50.640
without having to worry about
any of the infrastructure

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.440
pieces that are behind the
scenes to operate that.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.290
That's between NetApp and
Google to handle that.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:05:01.050
So there's really no need to
manage, patch, scale, maintain,

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.110
do any of the
infrastructure-related tasks.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.450
There's three different
service levels

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.190
that we offer cloud volumes on.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.610
And the next slide will go
into some of those details.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.360
But each of those
service levels is really

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.670
just a simple NFS or SMB
endpoint in a no-ops fashion.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:21.070
The second element is really
around native user experience.

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:23.910
So when customers go
and provision, manage,

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.160
and monitor cloud
volumes, they actually

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.370
get an interface,
a user interface

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.680
that is identical to
how they would provision

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:32.934
any other Google service.

00:05:32.934 --> 00:05:35.350
MANVINDER SINGH: So I just
want to stress upon that point,

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:39.030
because when customers use
partner services on GCP,

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:41.809
they're not used to actually
using the same interface.

00:05:41.809 --> 00:05:44.100
So this would look and feel
just like a Google service?

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:44.850
ALIM KARIM: Exactly.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.620
And really what we
wanted to focus on here

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.470
is keeping that customer
experience seamless,

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:51.720
keeping them within
the Google ecosystem.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.540
And therefore, they don't have
to learn other interfaces,

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.600
and just use what
they're used to.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.730
The next point around rich
data management-- this

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.280
is where NetApp brings
its years of capabilities

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.000
around being able to rapidly
create snapshots, clones,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.820
and be able to synchronize
data for all of the data that

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.220
lives in Cloud Volumes.

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:14.640
Support is another area that
we hear from customers about,

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.090
in that to complete
that customer lifecycle,

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.550
the ability to have integrated
support is very, very

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:21.100
important.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:23.250
So with Cloud
Volumes, L1 support

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.650
is actually managed by
the Google Cloud team,

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:30.140
with similar SLOs as
other GCP services.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:33.320
On the right-hand side, around
pay-as-you-go and billing--

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:36.020
so again, being a
cloud service, you only

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.510
pay for the amount of
capacity you provision.

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.880
And that provision
capacity can vary over

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.596
the course of a day, over
the course of a week,

00:06:43.596 --> 00:06:44.720
over the course of a month.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.060
And you just pay for
the average consumption

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.190
over the course of a month.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.660
With that is the notion
of integrated billing.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:53.590
So even though this
is a NetApp service,

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:55.490
it's very deeply
integrated with Google.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:58.610
So you as a customer get
your bill from Google,

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:01.100
just as part of your
regular GCP invoice,

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.620
with the consumption of Cloud
Volumes as a single line

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.380
item on that bill.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:08.810
Hybrid enabled-- I talked
a lot about customers

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.670
being able to move data between
on-premise and the cloud.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:14.990
So we're offering services
to be able to sync data

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:18.800
from on-prem, making it
easier for customers to move

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.930
to Cloud Volumes in GCP.

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:23.000
And then finally, we
have a plan to roll out

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.220
this service across
the globe, starting

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:28.740
with some regions in the US,
and expanding out as we go.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.280
MANVINDER SINGH: So all these
points taken together, Alim--

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.800
with the native user experience,
with integrated support,

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.290
with billing being
integrated-- as a user,

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:38.240
I may not even have
to talk to NetApp

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.500
or interface with NetApp
while consuming the service.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:40.910
Right?

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:41.480
ALIM KARIM: Absolutely.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:41.980
Right.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:43.680
And this is, again,
to the earlier point

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.350
that we were making
is we want customers

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:47.660
to stay within
the GCP ecosystem,

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.420
and reduce the friction that it
takes to consume this service.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:51.336
MANVINDER SINGH: Cool.

00:07:54.654 --> 00:07:56.570
ALIM KARIM: Now lets
talk about service level.

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:58.810
So cloud volumes are
really available in,

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.030
let's call it three
different flavors.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:04.570
And these flavors are identical,
in the sense that you can offer

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:07.510
or you can consume both
NFS and SMB protocols

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:09.310
across any service level.

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.260
You get the advanced
data management features

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:13.120
across all service
levels, and the ability

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.670
to take snapshots,
do rapid copies,

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:18.410
do cloning, or do
data synchronization.

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.200
But what's different
with these service

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.850
levels is the
amount of throughput

00:08:21.850 --> 00:08:25.750
that you get per terabyte of
capacity that you provision.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:27.160
So we have standard.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:28.030
We have premium.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:29.500
We have extreme.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:31.690
Standard offers
customers 16 megabytes

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:33.429
per terabyte provision.

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:35.950
Premium is 64
megabytes per terabyte.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:40.059
And then extreme is double that,
at 128 megabytes per terabyte.

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:41.740
There are three
different price points

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.560
to go along with these three
different service levels.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.140
And really, why we have
these three flavors,

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:49.150
as the use cases
indicate, we intend

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:51.190
customers to be able to
use different service

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:53.710
levels for different
kinds of workloads.

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:55.750
So in the case of
standard, they're

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:59.249
likely going to use things like
file backup or web serving.

00:08:59.249 --> 00:09:00.790
In the case of
premium, they're going

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:03.700
to use it for virtualized
applications, potentially

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.500
general-purpose databases.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:07.120
And then on the
extreme side, really,

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:10.600
is where use cases like
high-performance computing

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:11.900
and OLTP come into play.

00:09:15.492 --> 00:09:17.450
MANVINDER SINGH: On
high-performance computing,

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:21.380
that can be so many different
use cases across protocols.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:23.300
There's EDA.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:25.300
There's financial-- we
have a lot of hedge funds

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:28.205
who want to do Monte Carlo
simulations, for example,

00:09:28.205 --> 00:09:30.110
and want NFS endpoints
to store the data.

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:32.272
There's genomic sequencing.

00:09:32.272 --> 00:09:33.980
Is that-- all of those
use cases, do they

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.064
fit into the extreme tier?

00:09:35.064 --> 00:09:36.146
ALIM KARIM: Yeah, exactly.

00:09:36.146 --> 00:09:37.630
Because those are
all-- typically,

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.910
you find areas that
require high throughput,

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:43.160
sometimes high number of IOPS,
and very, very low latency.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:45.067
So those are good fits
for the extreme tier.

00:09:45.067 --> 00:09:46.900
MANVINDER SINGH: And
if I start with extreme

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:50.740
care, and I see that the
performance is already

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:54.640
very good for my use case, and I
may want a lower-priced option,

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.260
can I move to the Premium Tier?

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:56.710
ALIM KARIM: That's right.

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:58.700
You can dynamically move
to the Premium Tier.

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:01.720
MANVINDER SINGH: Awesome.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.520
ALIM KARIM: So let's
say you're a customer,

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:06.700
and you want to consume
the Cloud Volume service.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:08.920
So what does the technical
architecture underneath it

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:10.040
look like?

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:11.890
So here we have a
simple diagram that

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:16.300
shows a standard Google customer
project on the left-hand side.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:19.360
In this project, we
have GCE instances

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:22.490
that have been provisioned
in the us-east4 region.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:26.680
Note that these instances could
be in any zone in us-east4.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:29.285
And they're in
within a given VPC.

00:10:29.285 --> 00:10:31.660
On the right-hand side is
where you have the Cloud Volume

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:32.160
Service.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.960
And in this case, the user
has used that interface

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:37.360
to provision three
cloud volumes.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:40.360
So on the top part, you'll
notice a dotted line,

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:43.090
and so there's a subscription
element between the customer's

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:45.190
project and the
cloud volume surface,

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:47.590
because each cloud
volume only exists

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:49.840
within the context
of a Google project,

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:53.470
just like any other Google
resource within a project.

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:56.320
On the bottom part
for each cloud volume,

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:00.370
we expose an export path, which
is then used by the instances

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.790
to mount that volume, and
access the data in that volume.

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:05.790
A few things to note here--

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:09.460
cloud volumes in this case
are accessible to any instance

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:11.530
in any zone within
a given region.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:12.740
That's number one.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:16.000
Number two is with the
use of export policies,

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:17.860
you can really
actually restrict which

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:20.980
instances are able to see
the data in cloud volumes.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:24.700
You can restrict it by a single
IP address for a single client.

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:27.190
You can restrict
it to an IP range.

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:29.440
And you can even tune
your export policies

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:32.620
so that you can allow certain
clients to read and write,

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.335
while other clients can only
read data in cloud volumes.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:42.090
Now let's talk through
a few use cases,

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.820
and what we've seen with
early customers that

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:46.530
are using cloud volumes.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:49.030
So the first one is around
continuous integration

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:51.420
and a software
development environment.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:54.900
We have a customer who has an
automated CI environment that

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:58.140
essentially deploys a
cluster with over 1,000

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:00.930
different containers that
come up in that cluster.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:04.230
And all of those containers
require access to a shared file

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:08.040
system to hold all the binaries
that the containers are going

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.260
to use for their build
jobs, as well as the output

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:14.460
to build artifacts once
the jobs are complete--

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:17.640
so again, a great use case
for Cloud Volumes, because you

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:20.050
get that shared file service.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:21.600
But the other key
thing to note here

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:26.340
is the KPIs in this environment
are the amount of unit tests

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:28.950
that these customers
can do in a given hour.

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:32.340
And that is directly correlated
with the amount of time

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:33.720
it takes to do a build.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:36.450
So it's not just about being
able to provide a shared file

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.240
system, but being able to
provide a shared file system

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:40.350
that is high performance.

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:42.240
So they need high
throughput, they

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:45.420
need low latency to be able
to keep those build times

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:48.840
to the minimum and do more
unit tests within a given

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:49.470
period of time.

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.570
The next use cases are
on video rendering.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:56.160
And this is one of
those classic use

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:58.650
cases with media processing
workflows, rendering,

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:01.890
transcoding, both from a
production and post-production

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:05.280
sense, that have tools that
are built around shared file

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:06.140
system.

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:07.980
And the access pattern
here, it's actually

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:10.354
very different from the earlier
use case we talked about.

00:13:10.354 --> 00:13:13.020
Here, access is very serial.

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:15.390
It is still high performance,
but it requires also,

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.100
again, very, very low latency.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:19.950
This is another good use case
we see for Cloud Volumes that

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:22.020
matches the tools that
are built for this use

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.830
case with the performance
that Cloud Volumes deliver.

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:30.350
So earlier,
Manvinder, you touched

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:33.410
on high-performance
computing and EDA.

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.270
So I'm going to use
the EDA example here.

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:39.080
And EDA is a complicated
environment on its own.

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:42.710
You have various phases around
specification, block design

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:46.160
and verification, synthesis,
formal verification,

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:47.370
and simulation.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:50.450
And each of these phases
has different tools

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:53.130
and requires different
file access patterns.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:55.520
But this is an
example of workloads

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:58.830
that have, for years,
relied on file services.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:00.980
So you have
applications and tools

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:03.572
that are designed with the
need for file services.

00:14:03.572 --> 00:14:04.280
And these tools--

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:05.990
MANVINDER SINGH: And
especially protocols like NFS.

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:06.823
ALIM KARIM: Exactly.

00:14:06.823 --> 00:14:10.010
And these tools are not
going to get re-architected.

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:11.510
They are going to
exist as they are.

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:14.600
It's just too hard to
rebuild these tools.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:19.010
And so when you have front-end
design systems in this EDA

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:21.270
space that resemble
software R&amp;D--

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:24.710
they're code heavy, they
use Verilog, they use VHDL.

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:26.630
And to your point,
these are tools

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:29.010
that, again, are very
tightly integrated with,

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:32.250
or require NFS.

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:36.170
And the KPI around here, again,
performance is of the essence--

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:38.690
the ability to complete
these workflows in time.

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:41.720
So this requires Cloud
Volumes specifically

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:44.480
to be able to handle large
volumes of small files

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:46.970
at very, very low
latency, especially

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:48.360
during the verification stage.

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:55.130
And the final use
cases are on databases.

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:57.740
And we have customers that
are using MySQL databases.

00:14:57.740 --> 00:15:01.420
And this could be
other databases as well

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:02.990
for transactional web platforms.

00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:04.990
And they are built-in
applications that actually

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:06.710
require scale-up performance.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:08.570
But the other unique
thing around this,

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:11.605
or not just around
performance, is this customer

00:15:11.605 --> 00:15:12.980
or this set of
customers actually

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:16.100
needs the ability to take
consistent point-in-time

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:19.470
reference copies off their
database for recovery purposes.

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:21.620
And again, the ability
to do this while

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:25.280
not impacting user experience,
still providing those high IOPS

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:27.920
and low latency on
a shared file system

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:30.060
are key for this
particular customer,

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:33.739
so another great use
case for Cloud Volumes.

00:15:33.739 --> 00:15:34.780
MANVINDER SINGH: Awesome.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:37.857
That's a lot of use cases.

00:15:37.857 --> 00:15:40.440
ALIM KARIM: So let's talk about
timelines, where we are today,

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:44.170
and where we're going to go with
Cloud Volumes in the future.

00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:47.089
So currently, the service is
available in private preview.

00:15:47.089 --> 00:15:49.380
We've been running the private
preview since early May,

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:51.120
since about when we
announced the partnership.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:52.470
MANVINDER SINGH: And
we have a few customers

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:54.970
that are trying Cloud Volumes,
and are really happy with it.

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:56.250
ALIM KARIM: That's right.

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:59.982
And we were offering this out
of the GCP us-east4 region.

00:15:59.982 --> 00:16:01.440
For those of you
that are watching,

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:03.570
and are interested in
participating in the preview

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:05.730
and expressing your
interest for Cloud Volumes,

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:07.480
please go to the link
that's on the slide,

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:12.330
netapp.com/gcppreview,
and register there.

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:14.872
In terms of future, the
next phases for the service

00:16:14.872 --> 00:16:16.830
are really taking it to
controlled availability

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:18.660
and general availability.

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:21.270
We're going to be making some
announcements around the Google

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:24.020
Next time frame, as well
as discussing which regions

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:25.890
the service rolls out to.

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:27.570
So I encourage
all of you, please

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:29.910
visit us at the
NetApp booth at NEXT.

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:32.804
Connect with our SMEs and
get some hands-on demos

00:16:32.804 --> 00:16:33.720
at the booth, as well.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:42.060
So to quickly
summarize, Cloud Volumes

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:44.580
for GCP basically are
simple to consume,

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:47.070
no-ops NFS or SMB services.

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:49.080
They offer our customers
high performance

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:50.550
and rich-data
management features,

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:53.100
and are very tightly
integrated into GCP

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:55.740
along different dimensions on
the user interface and control

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:59.100
plane side, with support,
and with billing.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:01.020
And ultimately, Cloud
Volumes through Google

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:05.130
allow our customers to migrate
existing workloads to GCP,

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.920
allow our customers to
develop new applications

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:10.890
on GCP that require
performant file storage,

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:13.349
and finally, allow our
customers to take advantage

00:17:13.349 --> 00:17:15.750
of the best-in-class
analytics and machine

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:17.160
learning capabilities
that Google

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:18.548
offers on unstructured data.

00:17:18.548 --> 00:17:19.589
MANVINDER SINGH: Awesome.

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:20.880
Thank you, Alim.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:21.630
All right, folks.

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.569
Stay tuned for live Q&amp;A. We'll
be back in less than a minute.

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:35.701
MANVINDER SINGH: OK, so we've
got some great questions here.

00:18:35.701 --> 00:18:37.450
I'm going to go a
little bit out of water,

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:40.800
Alim, just to touch upon the
stuff you just talked about.

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:43.820
So we talked about how a lot
of the features that customers

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:47.510
are used to on-premise,
with NetApp in many cases,

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:49.520
are now available in the cloud.

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:51.980
But the question that we're
getting a lot from our audience

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:54.710
is, how do I get that
data into cloud volumes?

00:18:54.710 --> 00:18:57.120
I may have it in NetApp
on a different platform

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:58.120
How do I move that data?

00:18:58.120 --> 00:18:59.578
ALIM KARIM: Yeah,
so we're actually

00:18:59.578 --> 00:19:02.870
going to use our Cloud Sync tool
to be able to allow customers

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.870
to move data from on-premise
into Cloud Volumes,

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:07.760
as well as between
Cloud Volumes.

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:09.200
And we can expect that.

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:10.410
It's not available now.

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:13.240
But it will be a roadmap item
that we add around the GA time

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:14.115
frame.

00:19:14.115 --> 00:19:16.490
MANVINDER SINGH: And that's
moving data over the network,

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:16.790
right?

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:17.915
ALIM KARIM: That's correct.

00:19:17.915 --> 00:19:19.070
That's over the wire.

00:19:19.070 --> 00:19:20.870
MANVINDER SINGH: And it doesn't
have to be NetApp on premise.

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:21.470
It can be--

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:23.360
ALIM KARIM: Yeah, and
this is the great thing

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:24.890
about a tool like Cloud Sync.

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:28.040
It can work with any
unstructured file format.

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:29.690
It could be NetApp storage.

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:31.070
It could be other storage.

00:19:31.070 --> 00:19:34.100
Basically, as long as that
storage exposes an NFS endpoint

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:36.800
or SMB endpoint, we're
able to then migrate

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:38.340
that data into Cloud Volumes.

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:40.640
MANVINDER SINGH: And
any limit on the amount

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:42.274
of data I want to
move-- terabytes,

00:19:42.274 --> 00:19:43.190
hundreds of terabytes?

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:45.148
ALIM KARIM: Yeah, so this
is where, again, it's

00:19:45.148 --> 00:19:46.790
a use case-dependent question.

00:19:46.790 --> 00:19:49.280
If you have small amounts of
data and you have good network

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:51.620
throughput, you're
able to move that,

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:54.320
using tools at Cloud Sync,
what we call "over-the-wire

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:55.670
synchronization tools."

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:58.250
But as you get into
larger, bulk data sets,

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:00.630
we're actually exploring
partnering with Google

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:04.160
on being able to move bulk
data in an offline fashion, so

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:07.250
more of a bulk data move,
and then followed by a sync

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:09.800
to get basically
hundreds of terabytes

00:20:09.800 --> 00:20:12.020
into GCP, into Cloud Volumes.

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:13.910
MANVINDER SINGH:
OK, cool, awesome.

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:16.430
So talking about the
protocols that the service

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:21.624
is going to support, NFS, there
are multiple versions, V3, V4.

00:20:21.624 --> 00:20:23.040
What's the thinking
on that front?

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.581
ALIM KARIM: So
initially, we're going

00:20:24.581 --> 00:20:27.540
to support NFS V3 and SMB V3.

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:30.390
And I think that kind of answers
a couple of those questions.

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:34.534
We're going to add NFS
V4 support down the road.

00:20:34.534 --> 00:20:36.200
And this is also an
area where we really

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:39.380
want to hear from our customers
on and what kind of protocols

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:40.310
they want to use.

00:20:40.310 --> 00:20:43.724
We're hearing a lot of demand
for SMB V3 and NFS V3, which

00:20:43.724 --> 00:20:44.890
is why we're starting htere.

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:45.350
MANVINDER SINGH:
That's where both

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:47.010
of the applications
[INTERPOSING VOICES] use,

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:47.340
right?

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:48.048
ALIM KARIM: Yeah.

00:20:48.048 --> 00:20:50.420
I think based on some of the
analysis we did, about 80%

00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:53.800
of the workloads that
are NFS based use NFS V3,

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:56.090
so that seemed like a
logical place to go.

00:20:56.090 --> 00:20:57.860
But again, we're
open to feedback,

00:20:57.860 --> 00:21:00.680
and hearing from customers on
use cases around interfaces V4,

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:01.722
and what they want to do.

00:21:01.722 --> 00:21:02.680
MANVINDER SINGH: Great.

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:03.360
Fantastic.

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:07.437
Is a cloud volume accessible
from multiple GCP projects?

00:21:07.437 --> 00:21:09.020
ALIM KARIM: So we
touched a little bit

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:11.840
on this presentation, but this
is a good question to bring up.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:15.830
So much like any other GCP
resource within a project,

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:17.760
this is a
project-level resource.

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:21.020
So a cloud volume
exists and is accessible

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:23.185
only within the
context of a project.

00:21:23.185 --> 00:21:24.560
So if you have
multiple projects,

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:26.480
Cloud Volume in
one project is only

00:21:26.480 --> 00:21:28.070
accessible to that project.

00:21:28.070 --> 00:21:29.750
Cloud Volumes in
another project is

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:31.580
only accessible to
that other project.

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:34.550
There is no sharing of Cloud
Volumes between projects.

00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:36.890
MANVINDER SINGH: OK.

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:39.355
What regions is the
service available in?

00:21:39.355 --> 00:21:40.730
ALIM KARIM: So
region-wise, we're

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:44.079
currently offering private
preview in us-east4.

00:21:44.079 --> 00:21:46.370
However, we're going to be
rolling out to other regions

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:47.300
in the US.

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:48.890
The timeline for
that is when we will

00:21:48.890 --> 00:21:51.440
talk about during the next
announcements in terms of which

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:52.732
regions are coming up and when.

